# UTC & Clock

All business timestamps are **UTC**. Keep time testable by injecting the clock instead of calling `DateTime.UtcNow` deep inside domain or calculators.

## Conventions

- Name UTC fields clearly: `CreatedAtUtc`, `FromUtc`, `ToUtc`, `StartTime`/`EndTime` stored/compared as UTC.
- Domain methods that depend on "now" accept an optional `DateTime? clockUtc` (see `Reservation`) or a required `DateTime` from the caller.
- Application handlers/calculators take `DateTime` (or optional range bounds) from the outside; prefer passing `DateTime.UtcNow` only at the composition edge (handler entry / background service), not inside pure logic.
- Normalize unspecified kinds to UTC when persisting (`DateTimeKind.Utc` / `ToUniversalTime()`).
- Unit tests **must** use fixed clocks — never live `DateTime.UtcNow` for assertions that depend on time.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — injectable clock
var now = clockUtc ?? DateTime.UtcNow;
space.EnsureValidReservationPeriod(period, now);

var stats = ManagerStatsCalculator.Calculate(reservations, spaces, now, fromUtc, toUtc);

// ✅ GOOD — test
var now = new DateTime(2026, 10, 17, 12, 0, 0, DateTimeKind.Utc);

// ❌ BAD — non-deterministic domain/calculator internals
public Money Calculate(...) => /* uses DateTime.UtcNow inside */;
```

## API / JSON

- Treat incoming date-times as UTC (or convert explicitly). Do not mix local server time with business rules.
